## Problem

Compilation in `mercantis core/mercantis_coreApp.swift` fails with:

```
mercantis_coreApp.swift:49:10 Instance member 'commands' cannot be used on type 'Scene'
```

## Root cause

In the `body: some Scene` `@SceneBuilder`, the `.commands { ... }` modifier is written as a standalone statement between scenes rather than being chained onto an actual `Scene` expression. The compiler parses `.commands` as a call on the type `Scene` itself, producing the "Instance member ... cannot be used on type 'Scene'" error.

Current problematic structure (see https://github.com/KevinBusuttil/mercantis.core.app/blob/main/mercantis%20core/mercantis_coreApp.swift#L17-L58):

```swift
var body: some Scene {
    WindowGroup { ... }
    #if os(macOS)
    WindowGroup("Visual Builder", ...) { ... }
    #endif
    #if os(macOS)
    .commands {                 // <-- error: not attached to a Scene
        CommandMenu("DocTypes") { ... }
    }
    #endif
}
```

## Fix

Attach `.commands { ... }` directly to a `Scene` (the primary `WindowGroup`). On macOS, `.commands` applies to the app's main menu regardless of which scene it's attached to, so attaching it to the main `WindowGroup` is the conventional and correct placement.

Merge the two `#if os(macOS)` blocks so that both the `Visual Builder` `WindowGroup` and the `.commands` modifier are gated together.

Desired result for `var body: some Scene`:

